Black hasn’t moved knight yet.  \n4. P: d4-d5 – advances pawn, blocks center, but allows Black’s e5 pawn to remain.  \n5. N: f3-e5 – attacks the pawn? But e5 is occupied by Black’s pawn, so Nxe5 is possible, but then Black recaptures with d6 pawn? Actually, if Nxe5, Black can recapture with d7 pawn (d6) or maybe queen? Nxe5 is a pawn sacrifice unless we calculate.  \n\nReason: En passant d4xe5 seems very strong: it captures a central pawn that just moved, opens the d-file for White’s rook after development, and gives White a passed pawn on e5? Not passed because Black has d7 pawn. But it gains a pawn immediately (if Black doesn’t recapture), and Black’s position is underdeveloped so recapturing might be awkward. After d4xe5, Black likely will play d6 attacking e5, but then White can support e5 with f4 or Nc3, or even develop bishop to f4. Also, en passant is a tactical opportunity that disappears after this move. It also opens lines for White’s pieces. Other developing moves are solid, but en passant is more forcing and gains a pawn. Therefore, I choose d4xe5 en passant.  \n\nAnswer: P: d4-e5"}
