: c3-e4**, attacking the queen. Since Black’s king is on f6, moving the queen away likely weakens Black’s coordination, and we gain tempo. Black queen’s only safe squares are like d8 or f7 (blocking king’s escape). Also, after Nxe4, we threaten discovered checks or pressure on d6/f6.  \n\n**Reason:**  \nIn the opening, developing with tempo is valuable. Here Nc3-e4 hits the queen, forces Black to lose time, and improves our knight position. This also opens possibility for future tactics involving our light-squared bishop or queen targeting f6.  \n\n**Answer:** N: c3-e4"}
