active diagonal c5 toward f8 and possibly pins something later. But less forcing.\n\nImmediate tactic: **R: f1-e1** now? Not check. Black might play …Kf7-g8 next.  \n\nBut the most forcing is **B: e7-d6** followed by **R f1-e1+** next move, forcing black king to move and possibly winning bishop or rook later.\n\nHowever I see **B: a6-c4** could also eye f7, but e7-d6 is more direct toward opening e-file.\n\nLet’s check **R: a5-f5** – attacks f7? f5 is not adjacent to f7. Not check. But might threaten rook f8.\n\nBut really the immediate **B: e7-d6** seems best because it clears e-file for rook without losing material and puts bishop on safer square.\n\n**Reason:**  \nWe want to exploit black king’s exposed position on f7. By moving bishop from e7 to d6, we free e7 for rook to check next move, forcing black king to move and possibly lose castling rights further, and we keep initiative. Black has few developing pieces, so pressure now could yield material or positional gains.\n\n**Answer:** B: e7-d6'}
