g the list: “B: e7-f6” is not listed. They listed “B: e7-d6, B: e7-c5, B: e7-b4, B: e7-a3, B: e7-f8, B: e7-d8, B: e7-g5, B: e7-h4” — no f6. So can’t capture bishop directly with that bishop. Actually Black bishop f6 might be guarded by king g6, so capture not safe? But anyway.\n\nSimplest winning plan: Centralize king and use rooks to checkmate. But to be safe and force mate quickly, I can double rooks on the 7th rank.\n\nOne direct try: R: h8-h7 (not check) threatens Rh6# next move? No, after Rh7, Black king g6, then Rh6+ Kf5, then maybe mate with rook a5-f5#? Not sure. Alternatively R: a5-g5+ forces king to h6 or f7. If Kf7 then R:h8-h7+ Kg8, Rg5-g7#? Maybe mate.\n\nActually, R: a5-g5+ is check (king at g6, rook g5 attacks g6). King moves: Kh6 or Kf7. If Kh6, then Rh8-h7#? No, from Kh6, rook h8 doesn’t give check. Rh7 is not check.  \nSo maybe not immediate mate.\n\nBut since we are completely winning, any reasonable move is fine. I’ll pick a move that improves position and doesn’t allow any counterplay.\n\nConsidering king safety: My king at g1, Black has bishop at f6 and king at g6 — no threats. So I can move king toward center: K: g1-f2 is fine.\n\nBut to be more aggressive: R: h8-h6+ forces king to move, then I can bring other rook into attack. That seems forcing.\n\nLet’s see:  \n1. R: h8-h6+ Kf7 (only safe square? Kh7?? Let’s see: If Kh7, then Rh6 is check, king goes to g8. Then I have Ra5-h5+ maybe? But not immediate win.) Actually, after Rh6+, if Kh7, then from h7, king can go to g8. Rh6 attacks h-file, so king can’t go to h8? h8 is attacked by rook h6? No, rook h6 attacks all squares on h-file except h6 itself, so h8 is attacked. So king from h7, legal moves: g8 (not attacked), maybe back to g6? g6 is attacked by rook h6? Rook on h6 attacks rank 6, so g6 is attacked. So from h7, king only g8. So after Rh6+ Kh7, next move I could play Ra5-h5+ Kg8, then Rh6-h8#? Not exactly because rook h5 covers h-file but h8 is occupied? Wait rook h6 is on h6, so h8 is em