ack bishop is on g5, can't capture f8. So black might move bishop to f4 or h6 etc. This develops pressure and possibly wins bishop if black blunders, but also might lead to trade of bishops, simplifying to king+pawns vs king, which is winning but slower.  \n- K: g1-f2: centralizing king, preparing to bring king to center to support pawn advances. Safe move.  \n- Pawn advance: b2-b4: gains space on queen side, prepares pawn majority.  \n- Pawn advance: g2-g4: attacks black king on h4? Actually g4 is attacked by white bishop on e2, but pawn move to g4 would push pawn to g4, attacking f5 and h5? But g4 is adjacent to black king on h4, so g4 would put black king in check? Wait: black king on h4, white pawn moves to g4: pawn attacks f5 and h5, not g4's own square. So moving pawn to g4 does not attack h4 (king is on h4, pawn attacks diagonally forward, so from g2 to g4, pawn would attack f3 and h3? Actually pawn on g4 attacks f5 and h5, not backward. So pawn on g4 does not give check to king on h4. But it does block g4 square, reducing black king's mobility. Also, after g4, black king might be more restricted. However, pawn on g4 is on light square, same as bishop on e2, so bishop defends it.  \n- Bishop moves like B: e2-d3 or B: e2-f3 develop bishop to more active squares.\n\nReason: The simplest winning plan is to centralize the king and advance pawns, especially the b-pawn and g/h pawns. However, black king is already somewhat exposed on h4. A direct attack might force the king back and create threats. B: f8-g7 immediately attacks the black bishop, which is one of black's two pieces. If black moves bishop, we might gain tempo; if black ignores, we might win bishop. Also, after B: f8-g7, black bishop could move to f4 attacking unprotected e2 bishop? Actually black bishop from g5 to f4 attacks e2 bishop (since f4 to e2 is diagonal). But e2 bishop is protected by king? King on g1 protects e2? Not directly. So B: f8-g7 might allow Bg5-f4 attacking our e2 bishop. That could