ctly! Yes, bishop at e3 would attack f2 knight (diagonal e3-f2). But knight could move away. Still, we can capture? Bishop move to e3 is valid (B: d2-e3). That immediately threatens knight. If knight moves, we might chase it.  \n\n3. **King moves:** K: f5-g6 maybe safer, away from knight’s possible attacks (knight at f2 attacks e4, g4, d1, h1 only within 2 squares, so king safe).  \n\nBut since we are White and likely winning, we should aim to capture knight quickly without allowing stalemate tricks.  \n\nQueen has many moves, but to attack knight, Q: g8-g2 attacks f2 knight vertically (same file g, rank 2) — yes, Q: g8-g2 is valid move (listed: Q: g8-g2). That pins knight? Actually queen at g2 attacks f2 knight because they are on adjacent files? Wait, g2 to f2: adjacent squares horizontally, queen attacks horizontally, so yes, queen at g2 attacks f2 knight. That would threaten capture next move.  \n\nBut is it safe? Knight at f2 attacks g4 and e4, not g2 (because knight moves L-shape: from f2 to g2: Δfile=1, Δrank=0 — not an L-shape; L-shape is 2 and 1). So knight cannot attack queen at g2. So Qg8-g2 is safe and directly threatens knight.  \n\nAlso B: d2-e3 attacks knight, but knight could move to d3 or h3 attacking queen at g2 if queen moves there first? But if we move bishop first, knight might move somewhere else.  \n\nGiven that Black has only a knight, we can just capture it with queen next move if we put queen on g2 now.  \n\nReason:  \nBest is to directly threaten the knight with a safe queen move, forcing Black to move knight, then we can chase with king/bishop/queen to eventually capture. Q: g8-g2 attacks f2, is safe from knight attacks, and also centralizes queen.  \n\nTherefore I choose Q: g8-g2.  \n\nAnswer: Q: g8-g2"}
